    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Small-scale structures along strike-slip fault zones in limestones exposed along the Bristol\r\nChannel, U.K., suggests that pressure solution plays a key role during fault nucleation and growth. Incipient shear zones consist of echelon veins. The first generation of solution seams forms due to bending of the intact rock (bridge) between overlapping veins. As the bridge rotates, slip occurs along the seams, causing centimetre-scale calcite- filled pull-apart structures to form and allowing fault displacement to increase. A second generation of solution seams forms at the tip of the sliding seams. As displacement increases further slip also can occur along this second generation solution seams, producing the third generation of solution seams as well as tail cracks at their tip. These three generations solution seams all contribute to the formation of individual fault segments. In the study area, sidestepping segments form contractional relays within which the fourth and subsequent generations of solution seams occur. The relays are breached by slip along these localized solution seams, eventually leading to the formation of a through-going fault with several metres of displacement.\r\n\r\nThe echelon veins, solution seams of various generations and tail cracks progressively fragment the fault zone material as fault slip accumulates. Slip planes nucleate on these pre-existing discontinuities, principally along the clay- enriched, presumably weak solution seams. This can be observed at a variety of scales and suggests that ModeH shear fracturing does not occur as a primary fracture mechanism, but only as a macroscopic phenomenon following ModeI (veins and tail cracks) and anti-mode I (solution seams) deformation appears that solution seams can play the same role as do micro cracks in localizing through-going slip planes. This micromechanicalmodel of faulting may be morewidely applicable becausethe characteristic structures have been described along other faults and shearzonesandin other rocktypes.",
